{"reason": "The execution shows a severely underfitting model with val logloss ~1.09 and accuracy ~40%, barely above random. The initial plan correctly identifies the root cause: the custom MultiResolutionAuthorClassifier has critical bugs (untrained proj_to_fusion layers, dimensional mismatches) and the hand-crafted features drown out the transformer signal. Similar successful experiments (e.g., 'I will merge the three code snippets...') show that a standard AutoModelForSequenceClassification achieves much better results (~0.3-0.5 logloss). The failed experiment ('I'll merge the data processing...') tried complex ensembles but still suffered from feature engineering noise. Therefore, the most promising changes are: (1) replace the broken custom model with a standard HuggingFace classifier in model_design, and (2) simplify training by removing differential LRs and adding cosine annealing in training_evaluation. Data processing should be simplified to remove hand-crafted features entirely, as they cause the problem.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all hand-crafted feature engineering functions (extract_syllable_features, extract_punctuation_rhythm, etc.) and the associated scaling/merging code. Only keep the text loading, stratified split, and tokenization steps. The rationale from the initial plan is that the 63-dimensional hand-crafted features are low-quality and numerically dominate the transformer output. Similar historical experiments show top performance from pure transformer fine-tuning without feature engineering. Implementation: delete all feature extraction function definitions and their calls, remove the scaler and feature concatenation, and ensure dataloaders only provide input_ids, attention_mask, and labels.", "model_design": "Replace the entire MultiResolutionAuthorClassifier with AutoModelForSequenceClassification from HuggingFace using microsoft/deberta-v3-large, with a dropout of 0.1. This eliminates all custom fusion layers, encoders, and the buggy proj_to_fusion. The initial plan identifies this as critical—the custom model has untrained projections and dimensional mismatches. Successful similar experiments show standard fine-tuning yields logloss ~0.3-0.5. Implementation: import AutoModelForSequenceClassification, instantiate with num_labels=3, and set hidden_dropout_prob=0.1. Remove the custom class and all references to stylo_dim etc.", "training_evaluation": "Increase epochs to 20, set learning rate to 1e-5 for all parameters (remove differential LRs), remove warmup, add cosine annealing scheduler with T_max=20, and keep dropout at 0.1. Set early stopping patience to 3. The initial plan supports these changes to improve convergence. Similar historical experiments (e.g., fine-tuning transformers) typically use a single low LR with cosine decay. Implementation: modify optimizer to use a single parameter group with lr=1e-5, replace scheduler with CosineAnnealingLR(T_max=20), and update NUM_EPOCHS=20, PATIENCE=3."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows a severely underfitting model with val logloss ~1.09 and accuracy ~40%, barely above random. The initial plan correctly identifies the root cause: the custom MultiResolutionAuthorClassifier has critical bugs (untrained proj_to_fusion layers, dimensional mismatches) and the hand-crafted features drown out the transformer signal. Similar successful experiments (e.g., 'I will merge the three code snippets...') show that a standard AutoModelForSequenceClassification achieves much better results (~0.3-0.5 logloss). The failed experiment ('I'll merge the data processing...') tried complex ensembles but still suffered from feature engineering noise. Therefore, the most promising changes are: (1) replace the broken custom model with a standard HuggingFace classifier in model_design, and (2) simplify training by removing differential LRs and adding cosine annealing in training_evaluation. Data processing should be simplified to remove hand-crafted features entirely, as they cause the problem.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all hand-crafted feature engineering functions (extract_syllable_features, extract_punctuation_rhythm, etc.) and the associated scaling/merging code. Only keep the text loading, stratified split, and tokenization steps. The rationale from the initial plan is that the 63-dimensional hand-crafted features are low-quality and numerically dominate the transformer output. Similar historical experiments show top performance from pure transformer fine-tuning without feature engineering. Implementation: delete all feature extraction function definitions and their calls, remove the scaler and feature concatenation, and ensure dataloaders only provide input_ids, attention_mask, and labels.",
    "model_design": "Replace the entire MultiResolutionAuthorClassifier with AutoModelForSequenceClassification from HuggingFace using microsoft/deberta-v3-large, with a dropout of 0.1. This eliminates all custom fusion layers, encoders, and the buggy proj_to_fusion. The initial plan identifies this as critical—the custom model has untrained projections and dimensional mismatches. Successful similar experiments show standard fine-tuning yields logloss ~0.3-0.5. Implementation: import AutoModelForSequenceClassification, instantiate with num_labels=3, and set hidden_dropout_prob=0.1. Remove the custom class and all references to stylo_dim etc.",
    "training_evaluation": "Increase epochs to 20, set learning rate to 1e-5 for all parameters (remove differential LRs), remove warmup, add cosine annealing scheduler with T_max=20, and keep dropout at 0.1. Set early stopping patience to 3. The initial plan supports these changes to improve convergence. Similar historical experiments (e.g., fine-tuning transformers) typically use a single low LR with cosine decay. Implementation: modify optimizer to use a single parameter group with lr=1e-5, replace scheduler with CosineAnnealingLR(T_max=20), and update NUM_EPOCHS=20, PATIENCE=3."
  }
}"}

